  "surprise": "Keep: 711 (719, 704, 710) against 704's 676 (682, 680, 667), up 24-43 on every seed, but not for the registered reason. The refocus never engaged: a target counted as saturated only with 4 distinct normalized proofs, and the fine-tune sharpens the policy onto one proof per target, so saturated counts after each round were 0, 1-14, 13-40, 64-81, 158-168, 270-301 and 397 (seed 2's seventh), k_rest rose only 32, 32, 32, 33, 34-35, 37-38, 40 and never approached 64, and the saturated-target calls cost 1-25 s a round (acceptance 0.89-0.93 there, yet still 11-187 new distinct proofs each). Rounds stayed at 704's cost, 226-284 s charged (sampling 45,000-48,000 at 162-213 samples/s; fine-tunes of 48-72 s in 583-750 token-budget steps, against 704's 76-118 s), so 6/6/7 rounds fit (704: 7/6/6). What the run is, then, is 704's flat sampling with 706's token-budget fine-tune, and it reached 1,214/1,203/1,225 RL targets (704: 1,191/1,189/1,173; 706's two seeds that took off: 1,239/1,193) from weak pretrained models: round-1 acceptance 3.1/7.1/3.7% at 8 thoughts (704: 18.9/11.4/5.4; 706: 5.3/4.8/2.8), with pretraining at 3,393-3,461 steps and, oddly, lower val loss than 704's (0.0773-0.0804 against 0.0843-0.0869, same code and seeds; held-out greedy 0.950-0.956 against 0.953-0.963), which did not translate into round-1 acceptance. Every seed took off under flat 32: acceptance 0.21 to 0.51 at round 3 (seed 0), 0.17 to 0.49 at round 3 (seed 2), 0.26 to 0.53 at round 4 (seed 1), where 706's 8 + 24 recipe stalled for 7 rounds on a 2.8% seed; this is consistent with 706's reading that diversity on solved targets drives take-off, but the test was not sharp since the two recipes also differ in the first pass's size. The dev gain is spread over lengths: seed 0 by bin 95/124/374/99/23/4 (704 seed 0: 95/123/356/86/19/3), so about +18 at length 9 and +14-18 at 10+, and RL targets of length 10+ ended at 87/95/99 (704 seed 0: 85). The most likely source of the +35 is the fine-tune itself (same 76,800 records, but 583-750 unaccumulated steps on homogeneous-length batches instead of 600 steps of 4 x 32), which 706 also credited with +20 to +45 on seeds past take-off; a seed's standard deviation of 10-25 covers part of it. Thoughts: pretraining val loss at 8 minus 0 was -0.0005, -0.0001, +0.0007; metric_diag 694/692/694 sits 12-25 below metric with per-sample eval accuracy 0.43-0.44 at 8 and 0.37-0.38 at 0, the no-thought path drifting a little under the 8-only fine-tune as in 704's seeds 0 and 1, not computation in the slots. So this keep is an RL-recipe gain and says nothing for test-time thoughts. Next: the saturation switch is wrong as written; to test the focus hypothesis, switch on the solved set once first-pass acceptance passes 0.5 (or count a target saturated at 2 proofs) so the ~300 unsolved targets actually get 64 samples. The gap to 590's 792 still looks like the pretraining: 708's models start RL at 3-7% acceptance, and the slot sequences cost about a third of 198's steps.",
